ncerned about is  what the various governmental decision  makers knew  when they knew it and why they did or  did not take certain action  trudeau friday seemed to say his  government has acted  appropriately from the very beginning  this government has made it very very  clear that  everyone in this country particularly  working for the government particularly  working for the canadian armed forces  deserves a safe and secure work  environment free  from any harassment as for vance he  denies the allegations  that he ever acted inappropriately  towards female subordinates  robin david aiken in ottawa thanks david  still ahead wuhan's rebound how the  region is now profiting  from the pandemic  exactly one year ago the city of wuhan  china was  under an unprecedented lockdown to  contain a mysterious new virus  as jeff semple explains life in the  epicenter of the kova 19 pandemic  has largely returned to normal  lately bin zhao has been feeling  homesick the calgary father grew up in  wuhan china  he was visiting his parents there last  year when the city became ground  zero for kovid 19. it was scary  i've never seen the city  like that ottawa even sent a plane to  rescue canadians trapped inside  including zhang's family i was happier  than winning a lottery  but one year later that pandemic script  has flipped  wuhan is roaring back to life back to  school and work  still wearing masks but breathing easier  knowing there hasn't been a confirmed  case of covid19  since last spring zhang even considered  taking his family back to wuhan  because you know be safer for our kids  safer for us  china's economy is also recovering  driven by manufacturing and an  unprecedented demand  for ppe 95 of canada's imported face  masks last year came from china  even while some local manufacturers sat  on the sidelines  when the pandemic's first wave crashed  into canada this bc based manufacturer  pivoted to produce  medical masks we're getting contacts  from all sorts of different people  requesting different having different  types of ppe the company president mark  rose says after six months  their masks remain in the warehouse  caught in red tape  the u.s government agency that certifies  n95 masks  is dealing with a backlog and without  that certification  rose says they've struggled to sell to  local health authorities  our products are collecting dust on the  shelves we just can't get these to  market  and and the government seems to be  buying materials from  other markets other markets like china  last year canada bought one and a half  billion dollars worth of chinese face  masks  millions of which were later recalled  over safety concerns  but those pandemic purchases helped to  fuel a record-breaking trade surplus for  china  the only major economy that actually  grew last year  the country where the pandemic appears  to have started is now profiting  from it jeff semple global news toronto  up next from the front line to the yard  line the super nurse drafted by the nfl  the weekend will be blinded by the super  bowl lights on sunday  the toronto born singer will be the  first canadian to perform at the  halftime show in nearly two decades  in 2003 shania twain opened the highly  anticipated event with  man i feel like a woman when the tampa  bay buccaneers battle kansas city in  sunday's big game about a third of the  crowd will be health care workers  the nfl has invited over 7 000 doctors  and nurses to the super bowl  as a reward for putting their lives on  the line during this pandemic  and as mike armstrong explains one nurse  will receive a special honor  right before kickoff it isn't that  you'll have a good seat for the big game  i still can't believe this is happening  nurse susie dorner's actually going to  be  on the field at least at the beginning  would you please toss the point  when the team captains take part in this  year's coin toss dorner will be  one of them we are extremely grateful  she was surprised last week  working from home buccaneers hall of  famer derek brooks turned up in a zoom  meeting  inviting dorner to be an honorary  captain oh my gosh  thank you so much i'm i'm so humbled  and honored i'm speechless  now as tampa general hospital has fought  the pandemic the  opportunities to smile have been hard to  come by  dorner is a covet icu nurse manager  there have been days and weeks that were  like a nightmare  this was a video put out in november the  past five months have  definitely taken a toll on my entire  team  is physically and emotionally exhausting  now the work dorner's done here at tampa  general hospital  isn't her whole story unfortunately  in the middle of all this she lost both  her grandmothers  it was natural causes not covid but one  of the grandmothers was a nurse in the  second world war  she was the inspiration that led dorner  into nursing  well losing her during covid was also a  lesson  i wasn't able to be there when she  passed away i wasn't able to say my  goodbyes so  one thing that i really pushed after she  passed away was  i got technology i got more ipads  knowing what she missed  dorner now tries to make sure patients  have what they need to talk to loved  ones  whether it's face timing or zooming to  just keep in touch  or at the end of life as happens too  often  i think there's hope on the horizon with  the vaccine dorner says she's starting  to see the light  at the end of the tunnel but what's  gotten her this far through the darkness  has something to do with football it's  her team  she says well the super bowl's also  given them all some smiles this week and  a welcome distraction as her teammates  help her practice  flipping coins mike armstrong global  news  tampa and that is global national for  this friday night i'm robin gill  tonight we leave you with more moments  from christopher plummer's remarkable  life  the canadian actor may have gone on to  hollywood but he spent half a century  with the stratfor shakespeare festival  he was indeed a legend thank you for  watching  i'll see you back here tomorrow good  night  you
 

